# The trace indent a CALL ON handler is given when it is delivered at a plain
# DO's own two clause boundaries, pinned on the tree-walker's own bytes.
#
# A plain `DO` has a header clause and an `END` clause, and a handler delivered
# at either of them starts its activation at whatever the block indent reads
# then. The two are not the same: the header clause has opened the block, so
# its handler runs one level in; the `END` clause has closed it again, so its
# handler runs back out at the `DO`'s own level. `Interp::settle_block_indent`
# is what each boundary calls, and its `open` argument is the whole of the
# difference -- a level being two columns.
#
# **One row per boundary, and each row is built so that only its own boundary
# delivers anything.** The first row queues nothing inside the block, so its
# `END` boundary has nothing to deliver; the second queues nothing before the
# `DO`, so its header boundary has nothing to deliver. Measured, flipping
# either `open` argument in `run_loop_with_header`'s `LoopKind::Simple` arm
# moves that argument's own row by two columns and leaves the other row
# byte-identical, on both engines.
#
# **The oracle differential cannot see this**, which is why the witness is
# here: `tests/support/mod.rs` normalises the width of a trace line's indent,
# and measured, `corpus_differential` in STRICT passes under either flip.
#
# What these rows do not pin: which argument carries the level. Only the
# settled indent reaches the handler, so `settle_block_indent(false,
# do_indent + 2)` at the header would produce these same bytes. Neither row
# contains a repeating loop, so neither says anything about the block indent a
# repeating `DO` settles.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE. Regenerate a
# row by running its program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 --
# ir_dual.rs refuses to run at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )

# Delivered at the DO's header clause. `zn = ra()` queues zx, its own boundary
# runs `h`, and `h` requeues zy -- which the next boundary owes, and the next
# boundary is the `DO`'s own. The block is open there, so `g:` echoes two
# columns in from the `DO`, ahead of the body's `say`.
program
trace r
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zn = ra()
do
say 'body'
end
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 4
out> g ran 5
out> body
out> after 1
err>      2 *-* call on user zx name h
err>      3 *-* call on user zy name g
err>      4 *-* zn = ra()
err>     10 *-*   ra:
err>     11 *-*   raise user zx return 1
err>        >K>     "RESULT" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>     12 *-*   h:
err>     13 *-*   say 'h ran' sigl
err>        >>>     "h ran 4"
err>     14 *-*   raise user zy return 1
err>        >K>     "RESULT" => "1"
err>      5 *-* do
err>     15 *-*     g:
err>     16 *-*     say 'g ran' sigl
err>        >>>       "g ran 5"
err>     17 *-*     return
err>      6 *-*   say 'body'
err>        >>>     "body"
err>      7 *-* end
err>      8 *-* say 'after' zn
err>        >>>   "after 1"
err>      9 *-* exit 0
err>        >>>   "0"

# Delivered at the END clause. Nothing is queued before the `DO`, so its header
# boundary has nothing to deliver; the body clause queues zx, runs `h` at its
# own boundary, and `h`'s requeue of zy is owed to the `END`. The block is
# closed by then, so `g:` echoes at the `DO`'s own level rather than the body's.
program
trace r
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
do
zn = ra()
end
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 5
out> g ran 6
out> after 1
err>      2 *-* call on user zx name h
err>      3 *-* call on user zy name g
err>      4 *-* do
err>      5 *-*   zn = ra()
err>      9 *-*     ra:
err>     10 *-*     raise user zx return 1
err>        >K>       "RESULT" => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>     11 *-*     h:
err>     12 *-*     say 'h ran' sigl
err>        >>>       "h ran 5"
err>     13 *-*     raise user zy return 1
err>        >K>       "RESULT" => "1"
err>      6 *-* end
err>     14 *-*   g:
err>     15 *-*   say 'g ran' sigl
err>        >>>     "g ran 6"
err>     16 *-*   return
err>      7 *-* say 'after' zn
err>        >>>   "after 1"
err>      8 *-* exit 0
err>        >>>   "0"
